WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:11.250
Take 1+2+4+8 and continue on and on adding the next power of 2 up to infinity.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:17.300
This might seem crazy, but there’s a sense in which
this infinite sum equals -1.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.440
If you’re like me, this feels strange or
obviously false when you first see it

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.600
but I promise you, by the end of this video you
and I will make it make sense.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.430
To do this, we need to back up, and you and I will walk through what it might feel like

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.750
to discover convergent infinite sums, the ones that at least seem to make sense

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.550
to define what they really mean, then to discover this crazy equation

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:45.100
and stumble upon new forms of math where it makes sense.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:50.290
Imagine that you are an early mathematician
in the process of discovering that 1/2 + 1/4

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:57.710
+ 1/8 + 1/16, on and on up to infinity, whatever
that means, equals 1, and imagine you needed

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:02.440
to define what it means to add infinitely
many things for your friends to take you seriously.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:07.570
What would that feel like? Frankly I have
no idea, and I imagine that more than anything

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:10.190
it feels like being wrong or stuck most of the time

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:15.259
but I’ll give my best guess at one way the successful parts of it might go.

00:01:15.259 --> 00:01:20.109
One day you are pondering the nature of distances between objects, and how no matter how close

00:01:20.109 --> 00:01:25.539
two things are, it seems thast they can always be brought a little bit closer together without touching

00:01:25.539 --> 00:01:29.579
Fond of math as you are, you want to capture this paradoxical feeling with numbers

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:35.020
so you imagine placing the two objects on the
number line, the first at 0, the second at 1

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.660
Then you march the first object towards
the second such that with each step

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.579
the distance between them is cut in half.

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:47.559
You keep track of the numbers this object touches during its march

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:53.080
writing down 1/2, 1/2 + 1/4, 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8, and so on.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:02:00.460
That is, each number is naturally written as a slightly longer sum with one more power of 2 in it. As such,

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:06.140
you are tempted to say that if these numbers approach anything, we should be able to write that thing as a sum

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.920
that contains the reciprocals of every power of
2. On the other hand, we can see geometrically

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:17.900
that these numbers approach 1, so what you want to say is that 1 and some kind of infinite sum

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.820
are the same thing.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.520
If your education was too formal, you’d write off this statement as ridiculous.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.860
Clearly you can’t add infinitely many things.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.840
No human, computer, or physical thing ever could perform such a task.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.740
If, however, you approach math with a healthy
irreverence, you’ll stand brave in the face

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.940
of ridiculousness and try to make sense out
of this nonsense that you wrote down, since

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.590
it kind of feels like nature gave it to you.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:48.490
So how exactly do you, dear mathematician,
go about defining infinite sums?

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:52.410
Well-practiced in math that you are, you know
that finding the right definitions is less

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:56.900
about generating new thoughts than it is about
dissecting old thoughts, so you go back to

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.910
how you came accross this fuzzy discovery.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:05.570
At no point did you actually perform infinitely
many operations.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:10.150
You had a list of numbers, a list that could keep going forever if you had the time, and

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.320
each number came from a perfectly reasonable
finite sum.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.780
You noticed that the numbers in this list
approach 1, but what do you mean by “approach”?

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:25.430
It’s not just that the distance between
each number and 1 gets smaller, because for

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:29.530
that matter the distance between each number
and 2 also gets smaller.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:34.370
After thinking about it, you realize what
makes 1 special is that your numbers can get

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.440
arbitrarily close to 1.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:42.160
Which is to say, no matter how small your
desired distance, 1/100th, 1/1,000,000th,

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.870
or one over the largest number you can write
down, if you go down the list long enough,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:53.040
the numbers will eventually fall within that tiny tiny distance of 1.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.360
Retrospectively this might seem like the clear way to solidify what you mean by “approach”,

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.790
but as a first time endeavor it’s actually
incredibly clever.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.930
Now you pull out your pen and scribble down
the definition of what it means for an infinite

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.090
sum to equal some number, say X.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.460
It means that when you generate a list of
numbers by cutting off your sum at finite

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:19.780
points, the numbers in this list approach
X, in the sense that no matter how small a

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.930
distance you choose, at some point down the
list all the numbers start falling within

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.920
that distance of X.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:31.560
In doing this, you just invented some math.
But it never felt like you were pulling things

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.770
out of thin air. You were just trying to justify
what it was the universe gave you in the first

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:40.090
place.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:44.060
You might wonder if you can find other, more
general truths about these infinite sums that

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.460
you just invented.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.280
To do so, you look for where you made any
arbitrary decisions.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.380
For instance, when you were shrinking the
distance between your objects, cutting the

00:04:53.400 --> 00:05:00.660
interval into pieces of size ½, ¼, etc.,
you could have chosen a proportion other than ½.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.040
You could have instead cut your interval into pieces of size 9/10 and 1/10

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.400
and then cut that rightmost piece into the same proportions

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.500
to get even smaller pieces of size 9/100 and 1/100

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.200
then cut that tiny piece of size 1/100
similarly, continuing on and on, you’d see that

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:26.630
9/10 + 9/100 + 9/1000 on and on up to infinity
equals 1, a fact more popularly written as

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.030
.9 repeating = 1.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.930
To all of your friends that insist that this does not equal 1, and it just approaches it

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:37.330
you can now just smile because you know that with infinite sums, to approach and to equal

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:40.780
mean the same thing.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:45.270
To be general about it, let’s say that you
cut the interval into pieces of size p and

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.280
(1-p), where p represents any number between 0 and 1.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:59.610
Cutting the piece of size p in similar proportions, we now get pieces of size p(1-p) and p^2.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:03.690
Continuing in this fashion, always cutting up the rightmost piece into those same proportions,

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:12.710
you will find that (1-p)+p(1-p)+p^2(1-p),
on and on always adding p to the next power

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:16.310
times (1-p), equals 1.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:24.129
Dividing both sides by (1-p), we get this
nice formula.

00:06:24.129 --> 00:06:28.520
In this formula, the universe has offered a weird form of nonsense

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:33.120
Even though the way you discovered it only makes sense for values of p between 0 and 1

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:39.480
the right hand side still makes sense when you replace p with any number, except maybe for 1.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:49.110
For instance, plugging in -1, the equation
reads 1-1+1-1 on and on forever

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:52.840
alternating between the two, equals 1/2

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:59.400
which feels both pretty silly and kind of like the only thing it could be.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:08.060
Plugging in 2, the equation reads 1 + 2 +
4 + 8 + ... = -1

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.240
something which doesn’t even seem reasonable.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.919
On the one hand, rigor would dictate that
you ignore these, since your definition of

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:19.669
infinite sums doesn’t apply in these cases.
The list of numbers that you generate by cutting

00:07:19.669 --> 00:07:31.210
off the sum at finite points doesn’t approach
anything.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:35.139
But you are a mathematician, not a robot,
so you don’t let the fact that something

00:07:35.139 --> 00:07:37.240
is nonsensical stop you.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:42.900
I will leave this sum for another day, so
that we can jump directly into this monster.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:47.919
First, to clean things up, notice what you
get when you cut off the sum at finite points:

00:07:47.919 --> 00:07:55.729
1, 3, 7, 15, 31. They are all one less than
a power of 2.

00:07:55.729 --> 00:08:03.259
In general, when you add up the first n powers
of 2, you get 2^{n+1} - 1, which this animation

00:08:03.259 --> 00:08:20.600
hopefully makes clear.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:24.430
You decide to humor the universe and pretend
that these numbers, all one less than a power

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:27.040
of two, actually do approach -1.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:32.009
It will prove to be cleaner if we add one
to everything and say that the powers of two

00:08:32.009 --> 00:08:35.699
approach zero.

00:08:35.699 --> 00:08:38.510
Is there any way that this can make sense?

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:42.890
In effect, what you are trying to do is make
this formula more general, by saying that

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:47.040
it applies to all numbers, not just those
between 0 and 1.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:52.400
Again, to make things more general you look for any place where you made an arbitrary choice.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.670
Here, that place turns out to be very sneaky.

00:08:55.670 --> 00:09:01.790
So sneaky, in fact, that it took mathematicians
until the 20th century to find it. It’s

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:05.040
the way that we define distance between two
rational numbers.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:11.630
That is to say, organizing them on a line
might not be the only reasonable way to organize

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.700
them.

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:20.540
The notion of distance is essentially a function
that takes in two numbers and outputs a number

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:24.690
indicating how far apart they are.

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:29.270
You could come up with a completely random
notion of distance, where 2 is 7 away from

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:35.060
3, and ½ is 4/5ths away from 100, and all
sorts of things, but if you want to actually

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:39.350
use a new distance function the way that you
use the familiar distance function, it should

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:42.420
share some of the same properties.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:46.620
For example, the distance between 2 numbers
shouldn’t change if you shift them both

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:53.640
by the same amount. From 0 and 4 should be
the same distance away as 1 and 5, or 2 and

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:59.210
6, even if that same distance is something
other than four as we're used to.

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:03.130
Keeping things general, the distance between
two numbers shouldn't change if you add the

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:05.160
same amount to both of them.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.740
Let's call this property “shift invariance”.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:13.080
There are other properties that you want your
notion of distance to have as well, like the

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:18.540
triangle inequality, but before we start worrying
about those, let's start imagining what notion

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:25.430
of distance could possibly make powers of
2 approach 0, and which is shift invariant.

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:29.959
At first you might toil for a while to find
a frame of mind where this doesn’t feel

00:10:29.959 --> 00:10:34.980
like utter nonsense, but with enough time
and a bit of luck you might think to organize

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:40.690
your numbers into rooms, sub-rooms, sub-sub-rooms,
and so on.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:46.980
You think of 0 as being in the same room as
all the powers of 2 greater than 1, as being

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:53.310
in the same sub-room as all powers of 2 greater
than 2, as being in the same sub-sub-room

00:10:53.310 --> 00:11:00.209
as powers of 2 greater than 4, and so on,
with infinitely many smaller and smaller rooms.

00:11:00.209 --> 00:11:05.029
It's pretty hard to draw infinitely many things,
so I'm only going to dwar four different sizes,

00:11:05.029 --> 00:11:10.040
but keep in the back of your mind that this
process should be able to go on forever.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:15.209
If we think of every number as lying in a
hierarchy of rooms, not just 0, shift-invariance

00:11:15.209 --> 00:11:20.690
will tell us where all of numbers must fall.
For instance, 1 should be as far away from

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:24.339
3 as 2 is from 0.

00:11:24.339 --> 00:11:29.740
Likewise the distance between 0 and 4 should
be the same as that between 1 and 5, 2 and

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:32.730
6, and 3 and 7.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:38.420
Continuing like this, you will see which rooms,
sub-rooms, sub-sub-rooms and so on successive

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:44.520
number must fall into.

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:49.450
You can also deduce where negative integers
must fall, where for example -1 has to be

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:58.700
in the same room as 1, in the same sub-room
as 3, the same sub-sub-room as 7, and so on,

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:04.220
always in smaller and smaller rooms with numbers
one less than a power of 2, because 0 is in

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:08.180
smaller and smaller rooms with the powers
of 2.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:13.570
So how do you turn this general idea of closeness
based on rooms and sub-rooms into an actual

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:15.390
distance function?

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:19.920
You can’t take this drawing too literally,
since it makes 1 look very close to 14, and

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:24.550
0 very far from 13, even though shift-invariance
should imply that they are the same distance

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:25.959
away.

00:12:25.959 --> 00:12:31.019
Again, in the actual process of discovery
you might toil away, scribbling through many

00:12:31.019 --> 00:12:35.170
sheets of paper, but if you have the idea
that the only thing which should matter in

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:40.730
determining the distance between two objects
is the size of the smallest room they share,

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:46.240
you might come up with the following: any
numbers lying in different large yellow rooms

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:53.760
are a distance 1 from each other; those which
are in the same large room, but not the same

00:12:53.760 --> 00:13:02.480
orange sub-room, are a distance ½ from each
other; those that are in the same orange sub-room

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:08.260
but not in the same sub-sub-room are a distance
¼ from each other.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:15.350
And you continue like this, using the reciprocal
of larger and larger powers of two to indicate

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:15.930
closeness.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:22.110
We won’t do it in this video, but see if
you can reason about which rooms other rational

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:28.500
numbers like 1/3 and ½ should fall into,
and see if you can prove why this notion of

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:33.149
distance satisfies many of the nice properties
we expect from a distance function, like the

00:13:33.149 --> 00:13:36.180
triangle inequality.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:40.740
Here I’ll just say that this notion of distance
is a perfectly legitimate one, we call it

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:45.040
the “2-adic metric”, and falls into a
general family of distance-functions called

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.810
the “p-adic metrics”, where p stands for
any prime number.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:53.740
These metrics each give rise to a completely
new type of number, neither real nor complex,

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:58.750
and have become a central notion in modern
number theory.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:03.810
Using the 2-adic metric, the fact that the
sum of all powers of 2 equals -1 actually

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:11.560
makes sense, because the numbers 1, 3, 7,
15, 31 and so on genuinely approach -1.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:16.910
This parable does not actually portray the
historical trajectory of discoveries, but,

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:21.070
nevertheless, I still think it’s a good
illustration of a recurring pattern in the

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:22.899
discovery of math.

00:14:22.899 --> 00:14:28.850
First nature hands you something that is ill-defined,
or even nonsensical. Then you define new concepts

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:33.740
that make this fuzzy discovery make sense,
and these new concepts tend to yield genuinely

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:37.279
useful math and broaden your mind about traditional
notions.

00:14:37.279 --> 00:14:42.610
So, in answer to the age-old question of whether
math is invention or discovery, my personal

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:47.540
belief is that discovery of non-rigorous truths
is what leads us to the construction of rigorous

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:52.170
terms that are useful, opening the door for more fuzzy discoveries, continuing the cycle.

